A||||17||rockcrawl||increasing traffic on roadways....over-population?||||1||
Z||000000||rockcrawl||12-22-2001||08:22 PM||||I'm a road service tech for Toyota Idustrial Equipment. I drive more than 30,000 miles per year in Eastern PA and NJ. In the past 5 years I have noticed a huge increase in the amount of traffic on the roads. I live in an area that is exploding with comercial/industrial development, and therefore also residential housing. Farm fields are dissapearing quickly. I'm seeing huge factories, wharehouses, and housing developments going up with very little being done to improve the roadways except for an additional traffic light here and there. I find myself sitting in traffic for 30 minutes where 5 years ago I was cruising right through the same intersection. I'm happy to see the increase in warehousing etc., because it means more work, but I'm not happy with the way the daily commuting is going. I used to think this was a great place to live because of all the employment oportunities, but I guess that's what eveyone else was thinking too, and now it's becoming crowded. I'm wondering if other people are seeing the same kind of thing in other parts of the country. Is the world becoming overpopulated? I think I should say goodbye to whatever open space there is left here.||||reg||1
Z||000001||FieroBUZZ||12-23-2001||09:43 AM||||Go look at Japan or somewhere where they are really packed in.  It's no where near overpopulated here yet.  The problem is that everyone wants to live in the same place, and the lefties running things concentrate on mass transit that is never used rather than maintaining the roads.<P>Here in Ottawa they found that something like 65% of the people syrveyed wanted better mass transit.  Guess who they surveyed? Regular bus commuters....duh!!!<BR>Gary||||reg||1
Z||000002||hugh||12-23-2001||10:12 AM||||I live in South Jersey,and am seeing the same thing.New Jersey is the most densley populated state in the country and to boot we are a corridor state between New York and the north to Washington and the south.We also get lots of polution from the west courtesy of the prevailing winds from that direction.You have to plan your driving around the rush hour traffic,which now takes up a good portion of the day. ||||reg||13
Z||000003||avengador1||12-23-2001||10:26 AM||||Try driving in Mexico city, Its totally nuts! Bumper to bumper all day long, and let's not even mention the smog. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Patrick's Dad||12-23-2001||01:56 PM||||Here in Boston, 'Rush Hour" lasts from Monday, about 4:30AM, to Saturday Morning, about 3:00AM. Non-rush hour traffic is just heavy.<P>We have the nations largest and most ambitious public works project going on at the moment. It's so bad that, as my family and I taxied out onto the runway of Orlando Int'l Airport, the pilot came on to announce, "For those of you who are wondering about the scope of the $15 Billion "Big Dig," you can look outside the left side of the plane and see the southern tip."<P>Here is, essentially the problem. Leftist control "experts" are trying purposefully not to fund infrastructure properly so that they point out all the gridlock everywhere, and throw more money at CAFE, traffic regulation, and other socialist control programs.<P>When the Northern Artery and Southeast Expressway (Rt 3 through Boston) were completed in the 1950's, they were to solve our traffic woes, carrying up to 55,000 vehicles a day. On the day they opened, almost 60,000 vehicles were using them each day. Now, the new Expressway, Ted Williams Tunnel, submerged Artery and Leonard Zakim/Bunker Hill Bridge will, by conservative projections, be overtaxed when they are complete. And the leftists will once again have created their own ammunition.<P>It bugs me. I want to move to Nevada. There, while on vacation, I made Laughlin (At the southern tip) to Las Vegas in about 1:10 in an underpowered Nissan. Here at home, it takes me about that to get to the North Shore, about 20 miles away.<P>It occurred to me that I didn't actually provide an answer (at least, my opinion) to the question put forth. Part of the answer is above; people move into the area that they want to be, and an incredible number of people are in the Boston - Washington corridor. The other part is that transportation is a "cheap" "necessity." Families have two and three cars in use, especially ones with older teens, so, for every hundred families, there are two to three hundred cars running around.<p>[This message has been edited by Patrick's Dad (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||rogergarrison||12-23-2001||03:45 PM||||I think the biggest problem is too many people have licenses that have no business driving at all. If we got rid of all the people that dont know the laws, cant see, no insurance, unroadworthy cars, or just actually dont know how to drive, wed lose about a third of the traffic. On our north side we recently expanded the freeway from 4 lanes to 8-10, now just a couple of months later all 8-10 are just as full as the 4 were.<P>Thats one reason, to relax, I head out of town on the little 2 lane roads whenever I can. No traffic, sometimes dont even hardly see another car.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||BN Boomer||12-23-2001||05:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroBUZZ:<BR><B>Go look at Japan or somewhere where they are really packed in.  It's no where near overpopulated here yet.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That only depends on how you choose to define overpopulated. I'd rather we not wait until we are as densely populated as Japan before we do anything about it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||12-23-2001||05:41 PM||||DelDOT's answer to every traffic problem? yet another traffic light. Rt 40 has gone from about 4 to more like 20 in just a few miles.<P>That or they help backdoor deals like Astra Zenica and now they are completely rerouting 141 north of Wilmington. 202/141 is a complete disaster. The new construction is not going to help. To bad for all the people that live up there. (We won't even get into Tyler McConnel Bridge which has obsolite at least 20 years ago...)<P>Other than park lands and huge private lands that simply haven't been sold yet, Delaware has an ever shringking amount of green space. Above the C&D cannal there's hardly anything left. 40 13 and 896 between the cannal and newark are all headed for being continuous shopping and condo/apt complexes. Not that County Gov't helps any.... One councilman is doing everything he can to pump up the values of his families property by pushing thru zoning changes. Code enforcement is completely run by morons... The County executive, Tom Gordon, answers when asked how the Radison on 95 could be built 1/3 larger than aprooved... (summarized) "What are they (code enforcement) suposed to do? measure it?"||||reg||1
Z||000008||rockcrawl||12-23-2001||05:58 PM||||So what can/should be done to prevent overpopulation? I have ideas, but they contradict my other beliefs. ||||reg||1
